I've tried to do some testing on this, but it has been made more
difficult as a result of the flux that the SBUS layer has been going
through in 2.6 lately (not complaining, but this bug has crept out of
it):

On sparc32 esp connected CDROMs (I'm testing with my ss20), when I try
to access the CDROM from Linux, the following spews from the kernel:

sr0: CDROM (ioctl) error, command: <6> Get configuration 46 00 00 00 00
00 00 00 20 00
sr: Current: Sense key: No Sense
    Additional sense: No additional sense information
isofs_fill_super: bread failed, dev=sr0, iso_blknum=16, block=32

I can confirm that the CDROM worked fine before (thanks to git
bisecting):

commit 576c352e89e57cfa6c9f493e549d10d86f60a0cf
Author: David S. Miller <davem@davemloft.net>
Date:   Fri Jun 23 15:55:45 2006 -0700
    [SBUS]: Rewrite and plug into of_device framework.
    I severely apologize, I was still learning how to program
    in C when I wrote this stuff 10 years ago...

    Signed-off-by: David S. Miller <davem@davemloft.net>

This bug is similar to:
http://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=393786

However, with the latest git tree (post merge of davem's sparc tree), I
get the above error instead of the DMA errors.

I'm not convinced this is toolchain related, as building git trees
before the of_device rewrite with the same toolchain works fine. Then
again, the new code could be exposing something.

I'm using: gcc version 4.1.1 20060629 (Red Hat 4.1.1-6)
Tested with kernel: 2.6.21-rc3-git9
Kernel config file is attached.

While this isn't technically a blocker for Aurora, its a rather big
annoyance. Not sure where to look on how to fix this one, but very
willing and able to test. First test will be with a gcc34 built kernel
to rule out compiler.

~spot

